  "arguments": [
    {
      "claim": "The threat or use of nuclear weapons violates Article 8(2)(b)(iv) of the Rome Statute because such weapons inevitably cause widespread, long‑term and severe damage to the natural environment that is clearly excessive in relation to any concrete and direct military advantage, even in an existential self‑defence scenario.",
      "reasoning": "Article 8(2)(b)(iv) criminalises an intentional attack launched with knowledge that it will cause environmental damage that is clearly excessive compared with the anticipated military advantage. Nuclear weapons, by their very nature, release immense heat, blast and prolonged radiation that cannot be confined in space or time; the factual description of nuclear weapons in the record shows that their effects are uncontrollable and persist for generations. Consequently, any state that threatens or employs a nuclear weapon knows that the resulting environmental harm will far outweigh any specific military gain, satisfying the proportionality element of 8(2)(b)(iv). The objection that the record does not quantify the damage is addressed by the inherent characteristics of nuclear weapons, which make the excessiveness of the damage a matter of scientific certainty rather than empirical case‑by‑case evidence.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "8(2)(b)(iv)",
          "quoted_text": "Intentionally launching an attack in the knowledge that such attack will cause incidental loss of life or injury to civilians or damage to civilian objects or widespread, long‑term and severe damage to the natural environment which would be clearly excessive in relation to the concrete and direct overall military advantage anticipated;"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The use of nuclear weapons breaches Additional Protocol I, article 54(2), because it attacks objects indispensable to the survival of the civilian population—such as water, foodstuffs and agricultural land—by contaminating, destroying or rendering them useless.",
      "reasoning": "Article 54(2) expressly prohibits attacks on objects indispensable to civilian survival for the purpose of denying them to the population, irrespective of motive. A nuclear detonation contaminates water sources, destroys crops, livestock and agricultural infrastructure, and renders large territories uninhabitable for generations. These effects match the description of “objects indispensable to the survival of the civilian population” listed in the article. Even if the state claims an existential self‑defence motive, the effect is the denial of essential sustenance, which the provision of the Protocol bars. Therefore, the employment of nuclear weapons constitutes a prohibited attack under AP I 54(2).",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "api-1977",
          "article": "54(2)",
          "quoted_text": "It is prohibited to attack, destroy, remove or render useless objects indispensable to the survival of the civilian population, such as foodstuffs, agricultural areas for the production of foodstuffs, crops, livestock, drinking water installations and supplies and irrigation works, for the specific purpose of denying them for their sustenance value to the civilian population or to the adverse Party, whatever the motive, whether in order to starve out civilians, to cause them to move away, or for any other motive."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

